From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Murray <81541646+RyanTheTide@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 05:46:08 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] clutter/frame-clock: Update now when even the minimum
 estimate is too late

When the update for the next presentation can't make it with the
maximum update time estimate, the fixed refresh rate scheduler tries the
minimum estimate. When even that is too late, the update is moved to a
later refresh cycle, timed by the minimum estimate, so it runs as late in
that cycle as it possibly can.

That also sends the frame callbacks for the update late. A client
drawing every frame, such as a fullscreen game or video player, then
commits its next frame too late for the following presentation as well,
and is put off by a whole cycle again. GTK 4 paces its frames on a grid
whose phase it takes from the first frame callbacks of an animation, so
once it lands in that phase it stays there. On a 120 Hz built-in panel in
a fixed refresh rate mode such a client gets 60-85 fps (intervals of
16.7 ms, mixed with 8.3, 12.5 and 20.8 ms), while the same client in the
variable refresh rate mode runs at 120 fps.

When the minimum estimate is too late too, update right away and aim at
the first presentation still in reach. A fixed refresh rate display
presents the frame at the same time as before, but the client gets its
frame callback early enough to commit its next frame in time.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Ryan Murray <81541646+RyanTheTide@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 clutter/clutter/clutter-frame-clock.c | 26 +++++++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 21 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/clutter/clutter/clutter-frame-clock.c b/clutter/clutter/clutter-frame-clock.c
index 12f4c90..56f375c 100644
--- a/clutter/clutter/clutter-frame-clock.c
+++ b/clutter/clutter/clutter-frame-clock.c
@@ -1239,12 +1239,28 @@ calculate_next_update_time_us (ClutterFrameClock *frame_clock,
 
               if (next_update_time_us < now_us)
                 {
-                  next_update_time_us =
-                    mtk_extrapolate_next_interval_boundary (next_update_time_us,
-                                                            now_us,
-                                                            refresh_interval_us);
+                  /* Too late even for the minimum estimate. Update now,
+                   * aiming at the first presentation that is still in reach,
+                   * instead of waiting for the latest possible time in a
+                   * later cycle: the frame is presented at the same time on
+                   * a fixed refresh rate display, but the frame callbacks go
+                   * out early enough for a client drawing every frame to
+                   * commit its next frame in time for the cycle after.
+                   * Otherwise such a client keeps committing too late, and
+                   * stays locked to every other refresh cycle.
+                   */
                   next_presentation_time_us =
-                    next_update_time_us + min_update_time_estimate_us;
+                    mtk_extrapolate_next_interval_boundary (next_presentation_time_us,
+                                                            now_us +
+                                                            min_update_time_estimate_us,
+                                                            refresh_interval_us);
+                  next_update_time_us = now_us;
+
+                  CLUTTER_NOTE (FRAME_CLOCK,
+                                "%s update too late for the next presentation, "
+                                "updating now for the one %ld µs later",
+                                frame_clock->output_name,
+                                next_presentation_time_us - now_us);
                 }
 
               if (next_presentation_time_us - max_update_time_estimate_us < now_us)
